#N canvas 248 385 820 520 12;
#X obj 40 20 inlet;
#X obj 400 20 inlet;
#X obj 40 101 f \$1;
#X obj 40 150 t f f;
#X obj 40 340 outlet;
#X obj 200 210 sel \$2;
#X obj 200 270 f \$1;
#X obj 239 242 + 1;
#X text 470 20 counter <min> <max>: vanilla stand-in for the Max/cyclone [counter] that this conversion transcribed without translating. It was loading as "... couldn't create" \, which means [counter 1 4] -- the object driving analyze_reader's whole four-stem batch loop -- silently did nothing \, and that loop could never advance past the first stem., f 44;
#X text 470 150 A bang on inlet 0 steps the count and outputs the value it held BEFORE stepping \, wrapping back to <min> once it passes <max>. The [t f f] fires right first \, so the next value is computed and stashed in [f]'s cold inlet before the current one leaves the outlet -- that ordering is the whole trick \, and is why this needs a trigger rather than a plain fan-out., f 44;
#X text 470 300 Inlets 1 and 2 exist only so that patch cords drawn against cyclone's counter still land somewhere \; cyclone used them for direction and carry \, neither of which anything in this repo sends. Inlet 3 sets the maximum \, which gnumbat-analyze does use -- it sends a "5" there \, and against the earlier single-inlet version that connection failed to load at all., f 44;
#X text 40 400 Note both [f \$1] boxes: a banged [f] outputs its creation argument \, which is how <min> is read. A message box [\$1] would have been the obvious way to write it and cannot work -- message-box dollars resolve against the INCOMING message \, never against creation arguments. Same trap that broke bpm_bar_resize~ and stem_timestretch~., f 44;
#X obj 40 55 route set bang;
#X obj 40 78 list trim;
#X text 40 530 RESUMING ACROSS SESSIONS. analyze_reader counts what is already in analysis_library.json and sends "set <n+1>" here -- 8 stems done means "set 9" \, so a file uploaded later is fetched from line 9 of stream.txt instead of re-analysing everything. Without [route set bang] that message lands on a bare [f] \, Pd prints "couldn't convert set to float" \, and the counter silently restarts at 1, f 44;
#X text 40 560 [list trim] added 2026-08-10: [route set bang]'s outlet 0 does not reliably naturalize a stripped one-atom remainder back to a bare float when the incoming "set N" arrived as a generic OSC/list-typed message (bridge_analyzeReader's counterSetOut -> [list prepend set] path) \, so [f \$1]'s cold inlet was receiving a "list N" message and throwing "inlet: expected 'float' but got 'list'" \, silently breaking the resume-across-sessions set and stalling the 4-stem batch loop. [list trim] forces it back to a bare float\; harmless no-op if the atom already arrives bare., f 44;
#X obj 300 55 print counter_unhandled;
#X text 300 85 REJECT-OUTLET FIX (2026-08-20) -- [route set bang]'s outlet 2 (reject: anything that is neither "set" nor "bang") used to be wired STRAIGHT into [f \$1]'s cold inlet (same destination as the legitimate "set N" path). Inlet 0 here does not only carry "set"/"bang" -- it sits on the SAME broadcast bridge_slicer's outlet 1 fans out to everywhere else (buffer_manager\, the GUI-hub status tee\, [select need_stemDurs]) \, so "sourceTrack"\, "sysMsg"\, "ready"\, "track_name" and "need_stemDurs" all land here too. Every one of them used to hit the reject outlet and get forwarded to a bare [f]'s cold inlet\, which only understands a plain float -- producing exactly the "select: no method for 'sourceTrack'" / "inlet: expected 'float' but got 'list'" console errors this project's Find-Last-Error pointed at. Rerouted to a print tap instead: visible \, harmless \, matches the buffer_manager_unhandled / registry_router_unknown_msg convention already used elsewhere in this codebase for exactly this situation., f 60;
#X connect 0 0 12 0;
#X connect 1 0 5 1;
#X connect 2 0 3 0;
#X connect 3 0 4 0;
#X connect 3 1 5 0;
#X connect 5 0 6 0;
#X connect 5 1 7 0;
#X connect 6 0 2 1;
#X connect 7 0 2 1;
#X connect 12 0 13 0;
#X connect 13 0 2 1;
#X connect 12 1 2 0;
#X connect 12 2 16 0;
